otin x \}\). This is the collection of every set that is not a member of itself. In other words, an element \(x\) is placed in \(\mathcal{R}\) exactly when the membership relation \(\in\) fails to link \(x\) to itself. It is tempting to call \(\mathcal{R}\) the set of all sets that are not members of themselves, but it quickly runs afoul of Russell’s paradox: assuming \(\mathcal{R} \in \mathcal{R}\) leads to \(\mathcal{R} 
otin \mathcal{R}\), and conversely if \(\mathcal{R} 
otin \mathcal{R}\) we get a contradiction. Thus \(\mathcal{R}\) is a candidate for a universal collection of sets that lack self‑membership, yet it is not itself a set in standard ZF set theory.  

On the other hand, the phrase “the set of all sets” is usually symbolized by \(\mathcal{U} = \{ s : s 	ext{ is a set} \}\). This would be a set that contains every set, i.e., a universal collection of all sets. Cantor’s theorem tells us that for any set \(X\) the power set \(\mathcal{P}(X)\) has a larger cardinality, so \(\mathcal{U}\) cannot be a set; it would lead to a cardinality paradox just as \(\mathcal{R}\) does. In practice we never actually form \(\mathcal{U}\); we instead work with proper classes like \(\mathcal{R}\) or the class of all sets, which lies outside the universe of sets. Hence the difference is that \(\mathcal{U}\) is an ill‑defined object that attempts to collect everything, while \(\mathcal{R}\) is a well‑defined subset (if it existed) of that alleged universal collection but quickly collapses under the paradoxes.

